    "content": "Explosions were heard overnight in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia's capital, according to three diplomatic sources on Thursday. The blasts come amid growing concerns about the potential spread of renewed conflict in the country's northern region. A source linked to the opposition alliance, which has been fighting government forces since last week, claimed responsibility for several drone strikes in the city, including some targeting the military headquarters. Federal troops and allied militiamen, meanwhile, have made fresh gains in the northern Tigray and Afar regions in ongoing clashes with the Tigray forces.\n\nThe explosions occurred shortly after Ethiopian authorities imposed a ban on drone flights in the capital. The ban, announced earlier this week, was justified as a security measure to prevent unauthorized aerial activity. However, the timing has raised questions about the government's ability to control airspace amid the escalating conflict. The opposition alliance, a coalition of armed groups opposing Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed's government, has been increasingly active in recent months, launching attacks in various parts of the country.\n\nThe conflict in northern Ethiopia, which began in November 2020, has already caused a massive humanitarian crisis, with thousands killed and millions displaced. The recent gains by federal forces in Tigray and Afar suggest a renewed push to regain territory lost to the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF) and its allies. The opposition alliance, which includes the TPLF and other groups, has vowed to continue fighting until the government meets its demands, including the lifting of a blockade on Tigray.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the developments in Ethiopia are of particular concern due to the country's proximity and historical ties. Many Somalis have family members and business interests in Ethiopia, and any escalation of conflict could disrupt trade routes and stability in the Horn of Africa. Additionally, Ethiopia's role as a key player in regional security, including its contributions to the African Union Mission in Somalia (AMISOM), means that instability there could have ripple effects on Somalia's own security situation. The diaspora has been closely watching the situation, with some expressing fears that a prolonged conflict could lead to a refugee influx and further strain on already limited resources.\n\nThe international community has called for a ceasefire and dialogue to resolve the crisis. The United Nations and African Union have urged both sides to prioritize the protection of civilians and allow humanitarian access. However, diplomatic efforts have so far failed to yield a breakthrough. The United States and European Union have threatened sanctions against those obstructing peace efforts, but it remains unclear whether such measures will influence the parties on the ground.\n\nAs the situation evolves, the Somali diaspora is advised to stay informed through reliable sources and to exercise caution when traveling to or communicating with contacts in Ethiopia. The explosions in Addis Ababa underscore the volatility of the current environment and the potential for further violence. The coming days will be critical in determining whether the conflict remains contained in the north or spreads to other parts of the country, with significant implications for the entire region.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/headlines/addis-ababa-explosions-ethiopia-drone-flight-ban/",
